\details{
A pair contributes one of four outcomes: both treatments respond, only treatment 1
responds (probability \code{p10}), only treatment 2 responds (probability \code{p01}),
or neither responds. The difference of the response probabilities of the two
treatments is \code{p10 - p01}, and only the numbers \code{a} and \code{b} of the two
kinds of discordant pairs enter the tests. The function supports the following tests:
\itemize{
\item The asymptotic McNemar test (McNemar), which refers
\code{Z = (a - b) / sqrt(a + b)} to the standard normal distribution without a
continuity correction, with \code{Z = 0} when \code{a + b = 0}
\item The exact conditional McNemar test (McNemar-exact), which refers \code{a} to the
binomial distribution with \code{a + b} trials and probability 1/2
\item The mid-p version of the exact conditional test (McNemar-midP), formulae (5)
and (6) of Fagerland et al. (2013)
\item The exact unconditional test of Suissa and Shuster (1991) (McNemar-uncond),
which orders the outcomes by \code{Z} and maximizes the null tail probability over
the probability \code{psi = p10 + p01} of a discordant pair, the test Z of Berger
and Sidik (2003)
\item The score test of Nam (1997) and Tango (1998) for the null hypothesis
\code{p10 - p01 = -margin} (Paired-Score), which refers
\code{(a - b + N margin) / sqrt(N (2 q01 - margin (margin + 1)))} to the standard
normal distribution, where \code{q01} is the maximum likelihood estimate of
\code{p01} under the null hypothesis, formulae (24) to (26) of Tango (1998). With
\code{margin = 0} it is the asymptotic McNemar test
}

The conditional distribution of \code{a} given \code{a + b} is symmetric under the null
hypothesis, so the usual conventions for the two-sided p-value of a conditional test
agree. The two-sided p-value of the exact conditional test is twice the smaller tail
probability, at most 1, and the two-sided mid-p value follows formulae (5) and (6) of
Fagerland et al. (2013). The two-sided versions of \code{'McNemar'},
\code{'McNemar-uncond'} and \code{'Paired-Score'} order the outcomes by the absolute
value of the statistic.

The test \code{'McNemar-uncond'} maximizes the tail probability of each outcome, a
polynomial of degree \code{N} in \code{psi}, over a grid of \code{n.grid} points. The
maximum on the grid can fall below the maximum over the unit interval. With
\code{ref.pvalue = TRUE} the grid is extended by points equally spaced on the arcsine
square-root scale and every local maximum on the grid is refined by a golden-section
search between its two neighbouring grid points, as in \code{\link{BinaryRR}}. With a
positive \code{bb.gamma} the maximization is restricted to the Clopper-Pearson
interval of level \code{1 - bb.gamma} for \code{psi} computed from \code{a + b}, and
\code{bb.gamma} is added, the confidence interval p-value of Berger and Sidik (2003).

The alternative \code{'less'} is handled by exchanging \code{a} and \code{b}. With
\code{alternative = 'equivalence'} the null hypothesis is tested by two one-sided score
tests, against \code{'greater'} with the margin \code{lower} and against \code{'less'}
with the margin \code{upper}, each at the level \code{alpha}, and the p-value of an
outcome is the larger of the two one-sided p-values.

The p-values are kept for the rest of the session and reused by later calls with the
same number of pairs and test, see \code{\link{bbssr-package}}.
}
